    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks that makes sense. so basically even if two people have the same password, their hashes will look totally different because of the salt right? can u give me a concrete example of how an attacker would try to use a rainbow table if we DIDN'T use salts?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Certainly! Imagine two users, Alice and Bob, both choose \"password123\" as their password. If anyone attempts to crack these passwords using a rainbow table, without salts, they'd find the hash for \"password123\" in the table and immediately compromise both accounts.\n\nHowever, if a unique salt (let's say \"salt123\") is added to each user's password before hashing. Alice's salted password becomes \"salt123password123\", while Bob's becomes \"salt123passwd123\". Even though both inputs start the same, once hashed, they produce entirely different hashes. Since rainbow tables can't account for unique salts, an attacker would need to compute or search for rainbow tables that include these variations, greatly slowing down or even preventing them from cracking the passwords. Thus, salting effectively thwarts attempts by attackers using precomputed hashes.",
      "source": "target",
